["S1","S2","S3"],"status":"PASS_BOUNDED_SOURCE_AND_CITATION_REVIEW","version":"API0-study-digest-source-review/1","word_count":90,"world_truth_certified":false},"source_input_ref":{"bytes":81709,"path":"data/recovery-studies-v1/digest-inputs/cloze_58515686f17296d84522d1ca/cdd8be71cb41778427b2e2fd89dad0618af46e7299e921ba66985845337dc00d.json","sha256":"cdd8be71cb41778427b2e2fd89dad0618af46e7299e921ba66985845337dc00d"},"source_map":[{"canonical_page_id":"page_f4e91be8f65a8efcc32b","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_f4e91be8f65a8efcc32b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":14,"end":705,"page_sha256":"332eb3e4253086a5f8048eff85cde7d8de65904732e421649253e0ffba65008f","start":454,"text":"Chief organizer of the disgruntled elite, which sees its long control over lucrative politico-economic power threatened by De la Guardia's moderate reforms, is probably former President Harmodio Arias, a publisher who wields extensive backstage power."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_f4e91be8f65a8efcc32b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":14,"end":654,"page_sha256":"332eb3e4253086a5f8048eff85cde7d8de65904732e421649253e0ffba65008f","start":623,"text":"former President Harmodio Arias"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_f4e91be8f65a8efcc32b","corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":14,"end":1238,"page_sha256":"332eb3e4253086a5f8048eff85cde7d8de65904732e421649253e0ffba65008f","start":454,"text":"Chief organizer of the disgruntled elite, which sees its long control over lucrative politico-economic power threatened by De la Guardia's moderate reforms, is probably former President Harmodio Arias, a publisher who wields extensive backstage power. One of his sons resigned this week as finance minister and another as ambassador to London. Arias has previously exploited the canal issue for political purposes and can be expected to use it again; one of his prot\u00e9g\u00e9s in the Foreign Ministry originated the 50-percent campaign. Nationalistic appeals and claims for larger canal payments could easily arouse the many thousands of restless, unemployed Panamanians who already tend to blame their country's critical economic situation and their own poverty on US control of the canal."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1958-03-29","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003600250001-4","earlier_page":14,"id":"S1","page_sha256":"332eb3e4253086a5f8048eff85cde7d8de65904732e421649253e0ffba65008f"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_1a835f2546153ef7b905","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1a835f2546153ef7b905","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-07-23","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003200190001-5","earlier_page":9,"end":328,"page_sha256":"2b720709ea0fdf56e6dc03d031c8a9d2dba846d05b460e5ab7ace8c63b82fd62","start":51,"text":"The American embassy in Panama believes First Vice President Diaz, who formally broke with the government coalition in late May, is \"ready to stake all\" at the antiadministration rally he has organized for 24 July in his attempt to oust President de la Guardia and succeed him."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1a835f2546153ef7b905","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-07-23","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003200190001-5","earlier_page":9,"end":116,"page_sha256":"2b720709ea0fdf56e6dc03d031c8a9d2dba846d05b460e5ab7ace8c63b82fd62","start":91,"text":"First Vice President Diaz"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_1a835f2546153ef7b905","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-07-23","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003200190001-5","earlier_page":9,"end":566,"page_sha256":"2b720709ea0fdf56e6dc03d031c8a9d2dba846d05b460e5ab7ace8c63b82fd62","start":51,"text":"The American embassy in Panama believes First Vice President Diaz, who formally broke with the government coalition in late May, is \"ready to stake all\" at the antiadministration rally he has organized for 24 July in his attempt to oust President de la Guardia and succeed him. De la Guardia is evidently concerned, for he warned in a surprise nationwide radio address on 20 July that the government would \"take all measures the circumstances demand against any attempt to overthrow the administration by violence.\""},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1957-07-23","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003200190001-5","earlier_page":9,"id":"S2","page_sha256":"2b720709ea0fdf56e6dc03d031c8a9d2dba846d05b460e5ab7ace8c63b82fd62"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_4baae79f44b17527bb10","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_4baae79f44b17527bb10","corpus":"cib","date":"1955-01-15","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A001800650001-0","earlier_page":10,"end":194,"page_sha256":"a607ea417dccc85d6344e008c5e8bbb25fe09aa706c060f34e4df8656b6f84ac","start":33,"text":"Also arrested were Guizado's son, one of his business associates, his minister of agriculture and commerce, and many followers of former president Arnulfo Arias."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_4baae79f44b17527bb10","corpus":"cib","date":"1955-01-15","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A001800650001-0","earlier_page":10,"end":193,"page_sha256":"a607ea417dccc85d6344e008c5e8bbb25fe09aa706c060f34e4df8656b6f84ac","start":163,"text":"former president Arnulfo Arias"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_4baae79f44b17527bb10","corpus":"cib","date":"1955-01-15","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A001800650001-0","earlier_page":10,"end":194,"page_sha256":"a607ea417dccc85d6344e008c5e8bbb25fe09aa706c060f34e4df8656b6f84ac","start":0,"text":"solution to the murder was near. Also arrested were Guizado's son, one of his business associates, his minister of agriculture and commerce, and many followers of former president Arnulfo Arias."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1955-01-15","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A001800650001-0","earlier_page":10,"id":"S3","page_sha256":"a607ea417dccc85d6344e008c5e8bbb25fe09aa706c060f34e4df8656b6f84ac"}],"text":"The leading suggestion, \u201csuch as former President Harmodio Arias,\u201d draws contextual support from the 29 March 1958 report, which calls him the probable organizer of a disgruntled elite threatened by de la Guardia\u2019s reforms and expects him to exploit the canal issue again [S1]. It does not connect him to incitement during the May riots. The alternative Diaz suggestion concerns the American embassy\u2019s assessment of his planned July 1957 challenge to de la Guardia [S2]; Arnulfo Arias appears only through the reported arrests of his followers in January 1955 [S3].","text_sha256":"ac97125f3de8337992f47fdd669a1cb71845388d2e3441723b0c86c0110dea67","text_utf8_sha256":"081e154ed088b13ccf10b6a9412861d095c3909bbf2d13bf651f60d4b808d23f","used_for_inference":false,"version":"recovery-study-postrun-digest-overlay/1"}